<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.14.2 Writing Equations for Point-Slope and Slope-Intercept</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00306</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.14.2 Writing Equations for Point-Slope and Slope-Intercept</md:title>
    <md:uuid>565c3da7-65cd-453e-8c01-941152cbdfbd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
<para><emphasis>Writing Equations Given Two Points</emphasis></para>
<para>For 1 - 3, use the point \((3,4)\) and \((-2, 5)\).</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="69c0e917-231f-4bb7-b26e-c3d1c4e8d7a2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> Find the slope of the line that goes through the two points. </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(-\frac15\)
        \(m=\frac{5-4}{-2-3}=\frac{1}{-5}\)
        \(m=\frac{5-4}{-2-3}=\frac{1}{-5}\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(-\frac15\)
        \(m=\frac{5-4}{-2-3}=\frac{1}{-5}\) 
        \(m=\frac{5-4}{-2-3}=\frac{1}{-5}\) </para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Find the equation in point slope-form of the line that goes through the two points. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\)
      \(y-4=-\frac{1}{5}(x-3)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write the equation from question 2 into slope-intercept form. </para>
    <para>(Hint: Solve for \(y\).)</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>
      \(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac{23}{5}\\y-4&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac35\\y-4+4&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac{3}{5}+4\\y-4+4&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac{3}{5}+\frac{20}{5}\\y&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac{23}{5}\end{array}\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<para><emphasis>Writing Equations in Slope-Intercept Form Given a Point and Slope</emphasis></para>
<para>For 4 - 7 use the point \((3, -2)\) and the slope -4.</para>

<!--Q#4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Substitute the slope into slope-intercept form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y= -4x +b\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Substitute the given point in for \((x, y)\) in the equation. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(-2= -4(3) +b\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="ee65d7ce-dddd-4fce-ba53-ad14f6eff791" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> Solve for \(b\) in slope-intercept form. </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 10</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is 10.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write the equation in slope-intercept form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y= -4x +10\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<para>With a partner, work to go directly from two points to an equation in slope-intercept form, without using point-slope form.</para>
<para>For 8 - 12, use the two points \((5, 2)\) and \((3, -1)\)</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="86409435-3d6d-4faa-ae2e-7ecb87fbf9a3" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> Find the slope between the two points. </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac32\)
        \(m=\frac{-1-2}{3-5}=\frac{-3}{-2}=\frac32\)
        \(m=\frac{-1-2}{3-5}=\frac{-3}{-2}=\frac32\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac32\)
        \(m=\frac{-1-2}{3-5}=\frac{-3}{-2}=\frac32\)
        \(m=\frac{-1-2}{3-5}=\frac{-3}{-2}=\frac32\)</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Substitute the slope into slope-intercept form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y= \frac{3}{2}x +b\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#10-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Substitute the one point in for \((x, y)\) in the equation. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(5= \frac{3}{2}(2) +b\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="8ae75025-f392-46a3-b8d9-27ec322c8f70" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> Solve for \(b\) in slope-intercept form. </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 2</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is 2.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#12-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write the equation in slope-intercept form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y= \frac{3}{2}x +2\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>
